 "circuits": [],
 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Differences between types",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "All eight types share the 10 A average and 250 A surge ratings, the 400 pF capacitance and the thermal figures. They differ in reverse voltage (VRRM and VDC 20 V to 200 V; RMS 14 V to 140 V), and the higher-voltage types have a higher forward drop and a lower leakage limit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Named uses are low-voltage high-frequency inverters, freewheeling, DC/DC converters and reverse-polarity protection."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current derating",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The 10 A average rating applies with a 60 Hz sine wave into a resistive load and holds up to a lead temperature of 100 °C; above that the allowed current falls in a straight line to zero at 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The surge current curve, for 8.3 ms single half-sine cycles, falls from about 170 A at one cycle to about 65 A at 10 cycles and about 13 A at 100 cycles. The rating table gives 250 A for one non-repetitive 8.3 ms half-sine."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Reverse current rises with reverse voltage and climbs by more than two decades from 25 °C to 100 °C. SK108B to SK1020B have the lower leakage limits and curves."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and soldering",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Moisture sensitivity level 1 per J-STD-020, lead-free peak temperature 260 °C. Mold compound rated UL 94V-0; RoHS compliant and halogen free. Terminals are solder plated and solderable to MIL-STD-750 method 2026. A band on the body marks the cathode end."
  }
 ],
